<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> most kids are ready for real ski lessons somewhere around age 3 to 5, when they can follow simple instructions and stay warm for an hour or two. Put them in ski school rather than teaching them yourself, keep days short and fun, dress them warmer than you think, and ski together once they can stop and turn on their own.</p></div>

<h2>When can kids start skiing?</h2>
<p>There's no single right age. Ski schools commonly start group lessons somewhere between ages 3 and 5, and some offer private lessons or snow play for younger toddlers. What matters more than age is readiness:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Can they follow two-step instructions from someone who isn't you?</li>
  <li>Are they potty trained, or does the program handle diapers?</li>
  <li>Do they have the leg strength to walk around in ski boots?</li>
  <li>Will they stay reasonably happy in the cold for an hour or two?</li>
</ul>
<p>If the answer to most of these is no, a day of sledding and snow play is a better first trip. Starting a year later beats a first experience that makes them hate the snow.</p>

<h2>Skiing with kids, age by age</h2>
<h3>Toddlers (about 2–3)</h3>
<p>Think snow play, not skiing. Some resorts offer toddler programs that mix a little time on skis with indoor play and naps. Short sessions, lots of breaks, and hot chocolate are the whole plan. A harness or edge-locking device can help some toddlers stand and glide, but keep expectations low.</p>
<h3>Preschoolers (about 3–5)</h3>
<p>This is when most kids start group ski school. Expect them to learn to slide, stop with a wedge ("pizza"), and maybe ride a carpet or slow beginner lift by the end of a few days. Half-day programs often work better than full days at this age.</p>
<h3>Young kids (about 6–9)</h3>
<p>Kids this age often learn fast. Many can link turns on green runs within a few days of lessons and start exploring easy blues. Full-day ski school is usually realistic, and it frees parents to ski their own terrain for part of the day.</p>
<h3>Tweens (about 10–12)</h3>
<p>Tweens can handle full days and real terrain. Some want to try snowboarding at this age; a lesson matters even more there because the first days on a board are humbling. See <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> for the basics they'll learn.</p>
<h3>Teens</h3>
<p>Teens can usually ski independently once they're confident, with a phone, a plan, and a meeting time. Many resorts run teen-specific lessons or freestyle camps, which tend to go over better than lessons with younger kids.</p>

<h2>Ski school vs teaching them yourself</h2>
<p>Ski school wins for almost every family. Instructors know how to teach kids in small steps, kids listen to them more than to parents, and lesson groups give kids friends their own age. Teaching your own kids often ends with sore backs and frustrated children.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Book early.</strong> Kids' programs fill up during holidays and weekends.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the right group.</strong> Be honest about ability so they aren't held back or pushed too fast.</li>
  <li><strong>Consider a private lesson</strong> for a nervous child or for siblings of similar ability to learn together.</li>
</ul>
<p>Parents who are beginners too can learn at the same time. See <%= link_to "ski lessons for adults", guide_path("ski-lessons-for-adults") %>.</p>

<h2>Keeping kids warm and happy</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Layers, not bulk:</strong> a base layer, a fleece, and a waterproof jacket and pants. No cotton.</li>
  <li><strong>Mittens over gloves</strong> for young kids; they're warmer and easier to put on. See <%= link_to "gloves vs mittens", guide_path("ski-gloves-vs-mittens") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>One pair of thin ski socks,</strong> pulled up smooth, with nothing else tucked into the boot.</li>
  <li><strong>A helmet and goggles</strong> that fit together without a gap at the forehead.</li>
  <li><strong>Snacks in every pocket</strong> and frequent warm-up breaks.</li>
  <li><strong>Hand warmers</strong> for cold days, placed on top of the hand inside the mitten.</li>
</ul>
<p>More detail in <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</p>

<h2>A realistic day with kids</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Get boots on at the lodging,</strong> or at a table near the ski school, not in a crowded parking lot.</li>
  <li><strong>Drop off at ski school early</strong> so lessons start on time and kids aren't rushed.</li>
  <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> many programs include it; otherwise plan a warm indoor break.</li>
  <li><strong>Ski together in the afternoon</strong> on terrain they learned that morning.</li>
  <li><strong>Quit while it's fun.</strong> Ending early on a high note beats one more cold, tired run.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Safety basics for families</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Teach kids the core of the skier responsibility code: stay in control, people below you have the right of way, and stop where you can be seen.</li>
  <li>Set a meeting point that's easy to find, like a specific lift base or lodge entrance.</li>
  <li>Put a card with your phone number in each child's jacket pocket.</li>
  <li>Make sure kids know how to ride a chairlift safely and to lower the bar.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Planning the trip</h2>
<p>Use the free <%= link_to "Ski Trip Planner", ski_trip_planner_path %> for the timeline and packing list, and read <%= link_to "family ski trip planning", guide_path("family-ski-trip") %> for choosing a resort and lodging.</p>
